1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,820
Around 240 BC, a scholar in Egypt worked out the size of the whole Earth.

2
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:12,560
He didn't travel round it. He used a shadow, a distance and one idea from geometry.

3
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:17,000
His answer: about 250,000 stadia.

4
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:20,920
And even today, we can't say exactly how close he came.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:27,100
This is the story of Eratosthenes, and of a number we still argue about.

6
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:40,870
Eratosthenes was born around 276 BC in Cyrene, a Greek city in what is now Libya.

7
00:00:40,950 --> 00:00:47,820
He studied in Athens for years. Then the king of Egypt, Ptolemy the Third, called him to Alexandria.

8
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:54,490
There he was put in charge of the Library of Alexandria, one of the largest libraries of the ancient world.

9
00:00:54,570 --> 00:00:57,830
The post also made him tutor to the future king.

10
00:00:57,910 --> 00:01:02,620
He wrote about mathematics, geography, poetry, history and the stars.

11
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:07,970
Admirers called him Pentathlos, like an athlete who competes in five events.

12
00:01:08,050 --> 00:01:11,910
Critics called him Beta: second best at everything.

13
00:01:11,990 --> 00:01:15,810
One thing he didn't need to prove was that the Earth is round.

14
00:01:15,890 --> 00:01:22,330
About a century earlier, Aristotle had pointed to the Earth's round shadow on the Moon during an eclipse.

15
00:01:22,410 --> 00:01:27,200
And he noted that some stars seen in Egypt can't be seen from further north.

16
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:32,470
Aristotle even reported a size for the Earth: 400,000 stadia.

17
00:01:32,550 --> 00:01:36,620
Eratosthenes found a way to measure it.

18
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:44,810
Far up the Nile, on Egypt's southern frontier, lay the town of Syene. Today it's Aswan.

19
00:01:44,890 --> 00:01:48,280
Syene was known for something that happened there once a year.

20
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:55,270
At noon on the summer solstice, the longest day of the year, upright sticks cast no shadow.

21
00:01:55,350 --> 00:02:01,760
Later writers also tell of a well at Syene, where the noon sunlight reached all the way down to the water.

22
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:04,260
The Sun was standing straight overhead.

23
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:10,710
Syene was taken to lie on the Tropic of Cancer, the furthest north the Sun can ever be overhead.

24
00:02:10,790 --> 00:02:18,000
But at Alexandria, on the coast, about 5,000 stadia further north, sticks still cast shadows at that noon.

25
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:24,120
If the Earth were flat, and the Sun far away, sticks in both places would cast the same shadow.

26
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:27,290
They don't. So the ground between them must curve.

27
00:02:27,370 --> 00:02:33,240
And how much it curves over that distance tells you the size of the whole Earth.

28
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:38,780
So how big was the shadow at Alexandria?

29
00:02:38,860 --> 00:02:44,400
According to Cleomedes, the shadow was read on a scaphe: a sundial shaped like a bowl.

30
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:49,520
A pointer stands at the bottom, and its tip sits at the centre of the bowl's curve.

31
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:53,290
At noon, the tip's shadow falls on the inside of the bowl.

32
00:02:53,370 --> 00:02:59,960
The arc from the foot of the pointer to the end of the shadow measures how far the Sun is from overhead.

33
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:04,280
At Alexandria, that arc was one fiftieth of a full circle.

34
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:07,430
In degrees, that's 7.2°.

35
00:03:07,510 --> 00:03:13,420
It's a small angle: a stick casts a noon shadow only about one eighth as long as itself.

36
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:18,570
At Syene, at the same noon, the shadow was nothing at all.

37
00:03:21,170 --> 00:03:26,740
Now the key idea. The Sun is so far away that its rays arrive almost parallel.

38
00:03:26,820 --> 00:03:31,360
Draw the Earth, with Syene at the top and Alexandria a little way round.

39
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:38,230
At Syene, a ray comes straight down the stick. Extend that stick downwards, and it reaches the Earth's centre.

40
00:03:38,310 --> 00:03:42,970
At Alexandria, extend the stick too. It also points to the centre.

41
00:03:43,050 --> 00:03:46,660
There, the ray meets the stick at 7.2°.

42
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:54,280
The Alexandria stick line crosses two parallel lines: the ray at Alexandria, and the ray down the stick at Syene.

43
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:59,050
When a line crosses two parallel lines, the alternate angles are equal.

44
00:03:59,130 --> 00:04:05,150
So the angle at the centre of the Earth, between the two cities, is also 7.2°.

45
00:04:05,230 --> 00:04:10,750
Syene and Alexandria are one fiftieth of the way round the Earth.

46
00:04:11,150 --> 00:04:18,570
And how parallel are the rays? Between those cities, they differ by less than a thousandth of a degree.

47
00:04:21,170 --> 00:04:24,310
Now he needed the distance between the two cities.

48
00:04:24,390 --> 00:04:27,380
Cleomedes gives it as 5,000 stadia.

49
00:04:27,460 --> 00:04:30,180
How was it measured? We don't really know.

50
00:04:30,260 --> 00:04:36,220
Egypt had bematists: professional pacers who measured land for farming and for taxes.

51
00:04:36,300 --> 00:04:40,020
Some historians think his distance came from their surveys.

52
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:46,090
A Latin writer, some 650 years later, says royal surveyors measured it.

53
00:04:46,170 --> 00:04:53,510
Either way: if 5,000 stadia is one fiftieth of the way round, the whole way round is fifty times as far.

54
00:04:53,590 --> 00:04:59,480
Fifty times 5,000: 250,000 stadia.

55
00:05:00,530 --> 00:05:05,230
A shadow, a distance, and the size of the whole Earth.

56
00:05:07,830 --> 00:05:11,840
That's the story as it's usually told. But where does it come from?

57
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:17,010
Eratosthenes wrote it up in a book, On the Measure of the Earth. The book is lost.

58
00:05:17,090 --> 00:05:22,030
Our main account comes from Cleomedes, who wrote a basic astronomy textbook.

59
00:05:22,110 --> 00:05:28,520
Nobody knows when he wrote it: somewhere between the first century BC and about AD 400.

60
00:05:28,600 --> 00:05:32,320
And Cleomedes says himself that his version is simplified.

61
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:39,660
His account talks about sundials, not the well. The well comes from other writers, like the geographer Strabo.

62
00:05:39,740 --> 00:05:41,750
There's even a second number.

63
00:05:41,830 --> 00:05:49,020
The Roman writer Pliny the Elder gives Eratosthenes' result as 252,000 stadia.

64
00:05:49,100 --> 00:05:52,710
Why the extra 2,000? Historians disagree.

65
00:05:52,790 --> 00:06:02,200
252,000 divides evenly by every whole number from one to ten. 250,000 doesn't.

66
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:08,120
Split into 360 degrees, it gives a neat 700 stadia for each one.

67
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:12,540
Some think he adjusted his figure to make calculations easier.

68
00:06:12,620 --> 00:06:20,260
Others think he defined a stadion of his own, from the size of the Earth, as Pliny's wording suggests.

69
00:06:22,860 --> 00:06:27,680
So how good was his answer? To check, we need his stadia in kilometres.

70
00:06:27,760 --> 00:06:33,350
A stadion was 600 feet. But a foot wasn't the same everywhere in the Greek world.

71
00:06:33,430 --> 00:06:40,120
Historians' estimates for the stadion run from about 150 to 210 metres.

72
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:46,570
The real distance round the Earth, through the poles, is about 40,008 kilometres.

73
00:06:46,650 --> 00:06:51,360
Here's what that range of stadia does to his 250,000.

74
00:06:51,440 --> 00:06:57,130
With a stadion of about 176 metres, he's about 10% too big.

75
00:06:57,210 --> 00:07:02,170
With about 185 metres, he's about 15% too big.

76
00:07:02,250 --> 00:07:13,490
But take Pliny's 252,000 and a short stadion, 155 to 160 metres, and he's within 2.4%.

77
00:07:13,570 --> 00:07:21,560
One study found about 157.7 metres from ancient distances, but critics say it's full of errors.

78
00:07:21,640 --> 00:07:27,530
So the famous claim that he was within 2% depends on choosing a short stadion.

79
00:07:27,610 --> 00:07:36,930
The method itself holds up. Repeated in 2012 with modern data, it gave 40,074 kilometres.

80
00:07:39,530 --> 00:07:44,140
Cleomedes' version also rests on simplifications. Let's check them.

81
00:07:44,220 --> 00:07:48,540
First, parallel rays. That one holds very well, as we've seen.

82
00:07:48,620 --> 00:07:52,510
Second, that Syene sits exactly on the Tropic of Cancer.

83
00:07:52,590 --> 00:07:58,800
In fact it's a little north of it, by less than a degree. Even there, the noon Sun wasn't quite overhead.

84
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:06,390
The tropic drifts, too. In Eratosthenes' time it lay about 0.3 degrees further north than today.

85
00:08:06,470 --> 00:08:11,180
Third, that Syene lies due south of Alexandria, on the same meridian.

86
00:08:11,260 --> 00:08:15,100
In fact it's about 3 degrees of longitude further east.

87
00:08:15,180 --> 00:08:21,370
And the Earth isn't a perfect sphere: it's slightly flattened, by about 0.3%.

88
00:08:21,450 --> 00:08:28,290
Still, the angle came out well. The two cities' latitudes really differ by about 7.1°.

89
00:08:28,370 --> 00:08:33,760
What we can't check is the distance, because we don't know his stadion.

90
00:08:36,360 --> 00:08:39,520
Measuring the Earth was only part of what he did.

91
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:44,890
In his Geographica, he mapped the known world on a grid of parallels and meridians.

92
00:08:44,970 --> 00:08:50,760
It named more than 400 cities with their locations, and it gave us the word geography.

93
00:08:50,840 --> 00:08:57,300
He measured the tilt of the Earth's axis, and worked out dates in history back to the fall of Troy.

94
00:08:57,380 --> 00:09:02,090
And his name lives on in mathematics, in the sieve of Eratosthenes.

95
00:09:02,170 --> 00:09:08,240
Write out the numbers. Cross out the multiples of 2, then of 3, then of 5, then of 7.

96
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:11,890
What's left are the prime numbers.

97
00:09:12,340 --> 00:09:19,280
Our earliest record of the sieve was written centuries later, and he may have named it rather than invented it.

98
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:27,770
Later writers used smaller figures. Ptolemy's Geography, centuries later, used just 180,000 stadia.

99
00:09:27,850 --> 00:09:37,140
Some seventeen centuries after Eratosthenes, Columbus read his work, but chose to believe in an Earth about a quarter smaller.

100
00:09:37,220 --> 00:09:40,190
So here's the whole idea, in one picture.

101
00:09:40,270 --> 00:09:45,610
No shadow at Syene. A shadow of one fiftieth of a circle at Alexandria.

102
00:09:45,690 --> 00:09:49,560
Parallel rays carry that angle to the centre of the Earth.

103
00:09:49,640 --> 00:09:55,100
Fifty times 5,000 stadia: 250,000 stadia round.

104
00:09:55,180 --> 00:09:59,590
How close was he? That depends on a unit we still can't pin down.

105
00:09:59,670 --> 00:10:04,960
But the idea was right, and it measured the whole Earth from two cities in Egypt.

106
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:09,210
Not bad for a man his critics called Beta.
